## Key Concepts
- CGO memory ownership and free semantics
- Returning malloc-allocated strings across the CGO boundary
- Safe Go wrappers around C allocations
- Concurrency: worker-pool batch dispatch with per-batch allocations

## Code Example
```c
// item.h
typedef struct {
    int id;
    double value;
    const char* name;
} Item;

int analyze_items(const Item* items, size_t n, char** out_json);
```

```c
// item.c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int analyze_items(const Item* items, size_t n, char** out_json) {
    if (!items || n == 0 || !out_json) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Build JSON manually (simplified)
    size_t needed = 128 + n * 64; // rough estimate
    char* json = malloc(needed);
    if (!json) {
        return -1;
    }

    int offset = 0;
    offset += snprintf(json + offset, needed - offset, "[{");
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (i > 0) offset += snprintf(json + offset, needed - offset, ",");
        offset += snprintf(json + offset, needed - offset,
            "{\"id\":%d,\"value\":%f,\"name\":\"%s\"}",
            items[i].id, items[i].value, items[i].name);
    }
    offset += snprintf(json + offset, needed - offset, "]");

    *out_json = json;
    return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests memory ownership across CGO boundaries, per-instance isolation, and error translation with minimal runtime plumbing; also exercises basic concurrency safety. 

## Key Concepts
- CGO memory management for heap-allocated returns
- Per-instance opaque handles and lifecycle
- Error code translation to Go errors
- Safe string conversion without leaks across calls

## Code Example
```c
// C header (kv.h)
typedef struct kv_store kv_store;
kv_store* kv_open(void);
void kv_close(kv_store*);
int kv_set(kv_store*, const char* key, const char* value);
int kv_get(kv_store*, const char* key, char** value_out);
void kv_free_value(char* value);
```

```go
// Go binding sketch (kv.go)
/*
#include "kv.h"
*/
import "C"

type KVStore struct{ ptr *C.kv_store }

func OpenStore() (*KVStore, error) { p := C.kv_open(); if p == nil { return nil, fmt.Errorf("open failed") }; return &KVStore{ptr: p}, nil }
func (s *KVStore) Close() error { C.kv_close(s.ptr); return nil }
func (s *KVStore) Set(key, value string) error { ck := C.CString(key); cv := C.CString(value); defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(ck)); defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(cv)); if C.kv_set(s.ptr, ck, cv) != 0 { return fmt.Errorf("set failed") }; return nil }
func (s *KVStore) Get(key string) (string, error) { ck := C.CString(key); defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(ck)); var val *C.char; if C.kv_get(s.ptr, ck, &val) != 0 { return "", fmt.Errorf("get failed") }; goStr := C.GoString(val); C.kv_free_value(val); return goStr, nil }
```

```go
// usage (main.go)
func main() {
  s1, _ := OpenStore()
  s2, _ := OpenStore()
  defer s1.Close(); defer s2.Close()
  go s1.Set("a","apple")
  go s2.Set("b","banana")
  go fmt.Println(s1.Get("a"))
  go fmt.Println(s2.Get("b"))
}
```

